1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:02,941
First, let's see what this matrix does.

2
00:00:02,941 --> 00:00:08,323
The first column, two one, is where the vector one zero lands.

3
00:00:08,323 --> 00:00:13,191
The second column, one two, is where zero one lands.

4
00:00:13,191 --> 00:00:15,426
Now try the vector one zero.

5
00:00:15,426 --> 00:00:19,588
It lands at two one, which is not on its original line.

6
00:00:19,588 --> 00:00:22,735
So one zero is not an eigenvector.

7
00:00:22,735 --> 00:00:27,926
Let's rotate a test vector around the origin and compare it with its image.

8
00:00:27,926 --> 00:00:33,764
Most of the time, the image points in a different direction, and there's a gap between them.

9
00:00:33,764 --> 00:00:36,455
But watch what happens here, at one one.

10
00:00:36,455 --> 00:00:38,102
The gap closes.

11
00:00:38,102 --> 00:00:40,294
The image is on the same line.

12
00:00:40,294 --> 00:00:42,999
And again here, at one minus one.

13
00:00:42,999 --> 00:00:45,558
These are our two special directions.

14
00:00:45,558 --> 00:00:48,308
Now apply the matrix to the whole grid.

15
00:00:48,308 --> 00:00:51,073
Notice that both lines stay where they are.

16
00:00:51,073 --> 00:00:54,573
Along the yellow line, everything is stretched.

17
00:00:54,573 --> 00:00:58,132
Along the green line, everything keeps its length.

18
00:00:58,132 --> 00:01:01,397
These lines are the skeleton of the transformation.

19
00:01:01,397 --> 00:01:04,382
The rest of the plane is just mixing the two.

